      "answer": "In the context of the PostgreSQL tutorial, \"atomic\" means that a transaction is an all-or-nothing operation. From the perspective of other transactions, the transaction either happens completely or not at all [2]. This ensures that if a system failure occurs partway through the operation, none of the steps executed so far will take effect, preventing inconsistent states such as debiting one account without crediting another [2]. Additionally, the intermediate states between steps are not visible to other concurrent transactions; updates made by an open transaction remain invisible until the transaction completes, at which point all updates become visible simultaneously [1][4].",
